//! member: false
//!
//! ls — list directory contents.
//!
//! References consulted: POSIX.1-2024 `ls`; GNU coreutils 9.11
//! `ls --help`, the info manual's `ls invocation` node and its
//! sub-nodes, and black-box runs of the GNU binary (the staged 9.11 on
//! linux x86-64, Homebrew's `gls` 9.11 on macOS arm64) under
//! `LC_ALL=C`; uutils' and toybox's `ls` as design references only.
//! Never GNU source. The probes behind every rule below are kasumi
//! `~/lanes/bu18/probe*.sh` and `notes/bu18-ls.md`.
//!
//! **What this `ls` is: GNU's `ls` when standard output is not a
//! terminal.** wolf 0.2.25 cannot ask whether descriptor 1 is a
//! terminal (`os_isatty` arrives in wolf 0.2.26, s215), so the two
//! terminal defaults — columns, and `?` for an unprintable byte — are
//! never taken: one name per line unless `-C`, `-x` or `-m` asks, names
//! printed as they are unless `-q` asks. Exactly what GNU does into a
//! pipe or a file, which is where scripts read it.
//!
//! GNU's rules, each measured against 9.11:
//!
//! - Operands: none means `.`. Files first, as one listing; then each
//!   directory, under a `NAME:` header when there was more than one
//!   operand (a failed one counts) or `-R`, a blank line before every
//!   header that follows output. `-d` lists the operands themselves.
//! - The last of `-1 -C -x -m -l` (and `--format`) wins; so does the
//!   last of `-a`/`-A`, and of `-S`/`-t`/`-X`/`--sort`. `--time=mtime`
//!   alone sorts by time.
//! - Sorting is by byte (the C locale); `-S` is largest first, `-t`
//!   newest first, `-X` by the text after the last `.`; every tie falls
//!   to the name, and `-r` reverses the whole order.
//!   `--group-directories-first` puts directories (a link to one
//!   included) first, and `-r` does not move them.
//! - `-F` appends `/` to a directory and `@` to a link; `-p` the `/`
//!   only. `-q` prints every byte outside printable ASCII as `?`.
//! - Statuses: 2 for an operand that cannot be reached or a directory
//!   operand that cannot be opened, and for a usage error; 1 for a
//!   directory `-R` meets and cannot open, for a dangling link `-L`
//!   must follow, and for a `--sort`/`--time`/`--format` word GNU's
//!   argmatch refuses; otherwise 0.
//!
//! **What wolf cannot see, and what that costs** (each a stated skip in
//! `tests/cases/ls.toml`, or a refusal by name, never an imitation):
//!
//! - **No `lstat`, no `readlink`, no mode, owner, link count, block
//!   count, atime or ctime** (wolf-lang#625; modes are #346, identity
//!   #536). Every path call FOLLOWS a link. So the long format and the
//!   options that imply it (`-l -g -n -o --full-time`), `-i`, `-s`,
//!   `-u`, `-c` and `--time=atime|ctime|birth` are REFUSED by name with
//!   status 2. And a link that resolves is taken for its target: `-F`
//!   and `-p` mark it as the target, `-S` and `-t` sort it by the
//!   target, `-R` descends through a link to a directory, and a
//!   command-line link to a directory is listed under `-d -F` as the
//!   directory. A DANGLING link is seen for what it is, because it is
//!   the one entry a directory lists and no path call finds: `-F` gives
//!   it its `@`, and as an operand it is listed rather than reported
//!   missing.
//! - **`-R` and a link to an ancestor.** GNU never descends through a
//!   link; this program cannot tell one, so before it descends it asks
//!   whether the directory is one of the directories it is already
//!   inside — the same size, modification time and listing, the test
//!   `pwd -L` uses (wolf-lang#536) — and skips it silently when it is,
//!   which is what GNU does to a link. A link to a directory that is not
//!   an ancestor is descended, where GNU's is not.
//! - **`fs_read_dir` sorts, and fails on a name that is not UTF-8**
//!   (wolf-lang#626): `-U`, `-f` and `--sort=none` are refused, and a
//!   directory holding such a name cannot be listed (status 2, said so).
//! - `-v`, `--sort=width`, the quoting styles (`-b -Q -N
//!   --quoting-style`), `-I --ignore --hide -B`, `-D`, `-Z`,
//!   `--color=always`, `--hyperlink`, `--zero`, `--block-size`, `--si`,
//!   `--author`, `-G` and `--time-style` are not written yet, and are
//!   refused by name (status 2) rather than ignored.

use bore

/// What the command line asked for.
struct Opts {
    all: int,
    directory: bool,
    recursive: bool,
    format: int,
    width: int,
    width_given: bool,
    tab: int,
    sort: int,
    sort_given: bool,
    time_given: bool,
    time_kind: int,
    reverse: bool,
    indicator: int,
    hide: bool,
    deref: int,
    group_dirs: bool,
    refused: str,
}

/// One name to list: what it is called, the directory it was listed
/// in (`""` for an operand, whose name is its path), and what a stat
/// said, once one was needed. The path is built only when a stat or a
/// diagnostic needs it, so a plain listing allocates none.
struct Entry {
    name: str,
    dir: str,
    known: bool,
    kind: int,
    size: int,
    mtime: int,
}

/// An entry not stat'ed yet.
fn entry(name: str, dir: str) -> Entry {
    Entry { name: name, dir: dir, known: false, kind: 0, size: 0, mtime: 0 }
}

/// The path that reaches `e`.
fn path_of(e: Entry) -> str {
    if e.dir.len == 0 {
        return e.name
    }
    joined(e.dir, e.name)
}

// The words for `format`, `sort`, `indicator`, `deref` and `kind`.
fn one_per_line() -> int {
    0
}

fn vertical() -> int {
    1
}

fn across() -> int {
    2
}

fn with_commas() -> int {
    3
}

fn long_format() -> int {
    4
}

fn by_name() -> int {
    0
}

fn by_size() -> int {
    1
}

fn by_time() -> int {
    2
}

fn by_extension() -> int {
    3
}

fn unsorted() -> int {
    4
}

fn by_version() -> int {
    5
}

fn by_width() -> int {
    6
}

fn no_indicator() -> int {
    0
}

fn slash() -> int {
    1
}

fn classify() -> int {
    2
}

fn follow_dir_operands() -> int {
    0
}

fn follow_operands() -> int {
    1
}

fn follow_always() -> int {
    2
}

fn a_file() -> int {
    0
}

fn a_directory() -> int {
    1
}

fn a_dangling_link() -> int {
    3
}

fn modification() -> int {
    0
}

fn main() -> int {
    let p = bore.parse(options(), env_args(), bore.permute())
    var out = bore.stdout()
    var o = Opts {
        all: 0,
        directory: false,
        recursive: false,
        format: one_per_line(),
        width: 80,
        width_given: false,
        tab: 8,
        sort: by_name(),
        sort_given: false,
        time_given: false,
        time_kind: modification(),
        reverse: false,
        indicator: no_indicator(),
        hide: false,
        deref: follow_dir_operands(),
        group_dirs: false,
        refused: "",
    }
    var i = 0
    while i < p.keys.len {
        let k = p.keys[i]
        let v = p.values[i]
        if k == "help" {
            bore.put(mut out, help()) else |_| {}
            return done(mut out, 0)
        }
        if k == "version" {
            bore.put(mut out, bore.version_text("ls")) else |_| {}
            return done(mut out, 0)
        }
        let status = take_option(mut o, k, v, p.given[i])
        if status != 0 {
            return status
        }
        i += 1
    }
    if !bore.ok(p) {
        eprint("ls: {p.error}\nTry 'ls --help' for more information.")
        return 2
    }
    if o.format == long_format() {
        o.refused = "the long format (-l, -g, -n, -o, --full-time) needs a file's mode, link count, owner and group, which wolf cannot read (wolf-lang#625)"
    }
    if o.time_kind != modification() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "-u, -c and --time=atime|ctime|birth need a time wolf cannot read (wolf-lang#625)"
    }
    if o.sort == unsorted() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "-U, -f and --sort=none need the directory's own order, which fs_read_dir does not keep (wolf-lang#626)"
    }
    if o.sort == by_version() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "-v and --sort=version are not written yet"
    }
    if o.sort == by_width() && o.refused.len == 0 {
        o.refused = "--sort=width is not written yet"
    }
    if o.refused.len > 0 {
        bore.warn("ls", o.refused)
        return 2
    }
    if o.time_given && !o.sort_given {
        o.sort = by_time()
    }
    if o.format == vertical() || o.format == across() || o.format == with_commas() {
        if !o.width_given {
            o.width = columns()
        }
    }
    var operands = copy p.operands
    if operands.len == 0 {
        (mut operands).push(".")
    }
    var failed = false
    let status = list_operands(mut out, o, operands, mut failed)
    if failed {
        bore.write_error(out, "ls", "write error")
        return 2
    }
    done(mut out, status)
}

/// `bore.finish`, but a failed write is status 2: measured for `ls >&-`
/// and `ls > /dev/full`, where the utilities `bore.finish` was measured
/// on say 1.
fn done(mut out: bore.Out, status: int) -> int {
    bore.flush(mut out) else |_| {
        bore.write_error(out, "ls", "write error")
        return 2
    }
    status
}

/// Apply one option, in command-line order, so the last of a set wins
/// as it does in GNU's. Answers a status to exit with at once (a word
/// GNU refuses there and then), or 0.
fn take_option(mut o: Opts, k: str, v: str, given: bool) -> int {
    if k == "all" {
        o.all = 2
    } else if k == "almost-all" {
        o.all = 1
    } else if k == "directory" {
        o.directory = true
    } else if k == "recursive" {
        o.recursive = true
    } else if k == "1" {
        // Measured: `-l -1` stays long, where `--format=single-column`
        // does not.
        if o.format != long_format() {
            o.format = one_per_line()
        }
    } else if k == "C" {
        o.format = vertical()
    } else if k == "x" {
        o.format = across()
    } else if k == "m" {
        o.format = with_commas()
    } else if k == "l" || k == "g" || k == "o" || k == "numeric-uid-gid" || k == "full-time" {
        o.format = long_format()
    } else if k == "format" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, format_words(), format_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error(
                "ls",
                v,
                "format",
                format_words(),
                format_meanings(),
                m == -2,
            )
        }
        o.format = m
    } else if k == "S" {
        o.sort = by_size()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "t" {
        o.sort = by_time()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "X" {
        o.sort = by_extension()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "U" {
        o.sort = unsorted()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "f" {
        o.sort = unsorted()
        o.sort_given = true
        o.all = 2
    } else if k == "v" {
        o.sort = by_version()
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "sort" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, sort_words(), sort_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "sort", sort_words(), sort_meanings(), m == -2)
        }
        o.sort = m
        o.sort_given = true
    } else if k == "time" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, time_words(), time_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "time", time_words(), time_meanings(), m == -2)
        }
        o.time_kind = m
        o.time_given = true
    } else if k == "u" || k == "c" {
        o.time_kind = 1
    } else if k == "reverse" {
        o.reverse = true
    } else if k == "F" {
        o.indicator = classify()
    } else if k == "p" {
        o.indicator = slash()
    } else if k == "file-type" {
        // `--file-type` is `-F` less the `*` for an executable, and wolf
        // cannot read the execute bits (wolf-lang#625), so here the two
        // are one.
        o.indicator = classify()
    } else if k == "classify" {
        if !given {
            o.indicator = classify()
        } else {
            let m = bore.argmatch(v, when_words(), when_meanings())
            if m < 0 {
                return bore.argmatch_error(
                    "ls",
                    v,
                    "classify",
                    when_words(),
                    when_meanings(),
                    m == -2,
                )
            }
            // Measured: `--classify=never` and `=auto` (off a terminal)
            // change nothing, not even an earlier -F or -p.
            if m == 0 {
                o.indicator = classify()
            }
        }
    } else if k == "indicator-style" {
        let m = bore.argmatch(v, indicator_words(), indicator_meanings())
        if m < 0 {
            return bore.argmatch_error(
                "ls",
                v,
                "indicator-style",
                indicator_words(),
                indicator_meanings(),
                m == -2,
            )
        }
        // `file-type` is a meaning of its own in GNU's list (3), and
        // the same as `classify` here (see `--file-type` above).
        o.indicator = if m == 3 then classify() else m
    } else if k == "hide-control-chars" {
        o.hide = true
    } else if k == "show-control-chars" {
        o.hide = false
    } else if k == "dereference-command-line" {
        o.deref = follow_operands()
    } else if k == "dereference" {
        o.deref = follow_always()
    } else if k == "dereference-command-line-symlink-to-dir" {
        o.deref = follow_dir_operands()
    } else if k == "group-directories-first" {
        o.group_dirs = true
    } else if k == "color" {
        if !given {
            o.refused = "--color=always is not written yet"
        } else {
            let m = bore.argmatch(v, when_words(), when_meanings())
            if m < 0 {
                return bore.argmatch_error("ls", v, "color", when_words(), when_meanings(), m == -2)
            }
            if m == 0 {
                o.refused = "--color=always is not written yet"
            }
        }
    } else if k == "width" {
        let w = line_width(v)
        if w < 0 {
            bore.warn("ls", "invalid line width: {bore.quote(v)}")
            return 2
        }
        o.width = w
        o.width_given = true
    } else if k == "tabsize" {
        let t = line_width(v)
        if t < 0 {
            bore.warn("ls", "invalid tab size: {bore.quote(v)}")
            return 2
        }
        o.tab = t
    } else if k == "inode" {
        o.refused = "-i needs a file's inode number, which wolf cannot read (wolf-lang#536)"
    } else if k == "size" {
        o.refused = "-s needs a file's block count, which wolf cannot read (wolf-lang#625)"
    } else if k != "human-readable" && k != "kibibytes" && o.refused.len == 0 {
        // -h and -k change only the sizes -l and -s print, and both of
        // those are refused; anything else left is not written yet.
        o.refused = "option '{shown_option(k)}' is not written yet"
    }
    0
}

/// How an option key reads on a command line, for a refusal.
fn shown_option(k: str) -> str {
    if k.len == 1 {
        return "-{k}"
    }
    "--{k}"
}

/// A `-w`/`-T` value: a decimal count, as many digits as given (a
/// count too large for an `int` is no limit, as GNU's is). -1 for a
/// value GNU refuses.
fn line_width(v: str) -> int {
    if v.len == 0 {
        return -1
    }
    for b in v.bytes() {
        let n = b as int
        if n < 48 || n > 57 {
            return -1
        }
    }
    v.to_int() else 0
}

/// The line width `-C`, `-x` and `-m` use without `-w`: `COLUMNS` when
/// it is a count (0 is no limit), else 80, warning about a bad one as
/// GNU does — only when a format that uses the width asked.
fn columns() -> int {
    let c = env_get("COLUMNS") else |_| {
        return 80
    }
    if c.len == 0 {
        return 80
    }
    let w = line_width(c)
    if w < 0 {
        bore.warn("ls", "ignoring invalid width in environment variable COLUMNS: {bore.quote(c)}")
        return 80
    }
    w
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- the listing

/// List every operand, files first, then the directories; answer the
/// status. `failed` comes back true when a write failed: the listing
/// goes on (GNU reports every operand it cannot reach, and the write
/// error once, at the end) but writes nothing more.
fn list_operands(mut out: bore.Out, o: Opts, operands: List[str], mut failed: bool) -> int {
    var status = 0
    var files = List[Entry]()
    var dirs = List[Entry]()
    for name in operands {
        var e = entry(name, "")
        stat_operand(mut e, o)
        if e.kind == a_dangling_link() && o.deref != follow_dir_operands() {
            bore.warn("ls", "cannot access {quote_always(name)}: {bore.no_such_file()}")
            status = 2
        } else if e.kind < 0 {
            bore.warn("ls", "cannot access {quote_always(name)}: {missing_reason(name)}")
            status = 2
        } else if e.kind == a_directory() && !o.directory {
            (mut dirs).push(e)
        } else {
            (mut files).push(e)
        }
    }
    let headers = operands.len > 1 || o.recursive
    var printed = false
    if files.len > 0 {
        let file_order = sorted(mut files, o)
        var bytes = List[byte]()
        let s = print_entries(mut bytes, files, file_order, o)
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
        bore.write_all(mut out, bytes) else |_| {
            failed = true
        }
        printed = true
    }
    // What is buffered goes out before the first directory, which each
    // write straight out of their own region.
    bore.flush(mut out) else |_| {
        failed = true
    }
    let dir_order = sorted(mut dirs, o)
    let ancestors = List[Entry]()
    for at in dir_order {
        let d = copy dirs[at]
        let s = list_directory(out, o, d.name, headers, mut printed, true, ancestors, mut failed)
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
    }
    status
}

/// List the directory `path`, and with `-R` everything under it, each
/// directory in a region of its own: what one listing allocates dies
/// with it, so `-R` holds the directories it is inside and no more.
/// `printed` says whether anything has been written yet, which decides
/// the blank line before a header; `operand` says whether a failure to
/// open it is an operand's (status 2) or a subdirectory's (1).
fn list_directory(
    out: bore.Out,
    o: Opts,
    path: str,
    headers: bool,
    mut printed: bool,
    operand: bool,
    ancestors: List[Entry],
    mut failed: bool,
) -> int {
    var status = 0
    region listing {
        status = list_here(out, o, path, headers, mut printed, operand, ancestors, mut failed)
    }
    status
}

fn list_here(
    out: bore.Out,
    o: Opts,
    path: str,
    headers: bool,
    mut printed: bool,
    operand: bool,
    ancestors: List[Entry],
    mut failed: bool,
) -> int {
    let names = fs_read_dir(path) else |e| {
        let why = match e {
            denied => bore.permission_denied(),
            not_found => bore.no_such_file(),
            utf8 => "a name in it is not UTF-8, which wolf cannot list (wolf-lang#626)",
            io => bore.io_error(),
        }
        bore.warn("ls", "cannot open directory {quote_always(path)}: {why}")
        return if operand then 2 else 1
    }
    var bytes = List[byte]()
    if headers {
        if printed {
            (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
        }
        push_text(mut bytes, shown(path, o.hide))
        push_text(mut bytes, ":\n")
    }
    printed = true
    var entries = List[Entry]()
    if o.all == 2 {
        (mut entries).push(entry(".", path))
        (mut entries).push(entry("..", path))
    }
    for n in names {
        if o.all == 0 && n.starts_with(".") {
            continue
        }
        (mut entries).push(entry(n, path))
    }
    let entry_order = sorted(mut entries, o)
    var status = print_entries(mut bytes, entries, entry_order, o)
    if !failed && bytes.len > 0 {
        bore.write_direct(out, bytes) else |_| {
            failed = true
        }
    }
    if !o.recursive {
        return status
    }
    var here = entry(path, "")
    here.known = true
    here.kind = a_directory()
    here.size = fs_size(path) else -1
    here.mtime = fs_modified_ms(path) else -1
    var mine = copy ancestors
    (mut mine).push(here)
    for at in entry_order {
        let e = copy entries[at]
        if e.name == "." || e.name == ".." {
            continue
        }
        if !e.known {
            continue
        }
        if e.kind != a_directory() {
            continue
        }
        let sub = descended(path, e.name)
        if inside(sub, mine) {
            continue
        }
        let s = list_directory(out, o, sub, true, mut printed, false, mine, mut failed)
        if s > status {
            status = s
        }
    }
    status
}

fn push_text(mut bytes: List[byte], s: str) {
    for b in s.bytes() {
        (mut bytes).push(b)
    }
}

/// The path of `name` inside `dir` as a stat needs it: GNU names an
/// entry of `.` by its bare name in a diagnostic.
fn joined(dir: str, name: str) -> str {
    if dir == "." {
        return name
    }
    descended(dir, name)
}

/// The path of `name` inside `dir` as `-R` prints it: `./sub`, `d/sub`,
/// and `d/sub` again for an operand written `d/`.
fn descended(dir: str, name: str) -> str {
    if dir.ends_with("/") {
        return dir + name
    }
    "{dir}/{name}"
}

/// Whether the directory at `path` is one this walk is already inside:
/// the same size, modification time and listing as an ancestor. See the
/// header: it is how a link to an ancestor is told from a subdirectory,
/// as far as wolf can tell.
fn inside(path: str, ancestors: List[Entry]) -> bool {
    let size = fs_size(path) else -1
    let mtime = fs_modified_ms(path) else -1
    for a in ancestors {
        if a.size == size && a.mtime == mtime {
            if same_listing(a.name, path) {
                return true
            }
        }
    }
    false
}

fn same_listing(a: str, b: str) -> bool {
    let la = fs_read_dir(a) else |_| {
        return false
    }
    let lb = fs_read_dir(b) else |_| {
        return false
    }
    if la.len != lb.len {
        return false
    }
    var k = 0
    while k < la.len {
        if la[k] != lb[k] {
            return false
        }
        k += 1
    }
    true
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ the stats

/// What an operand is. A path no call reaches may still be a DANGLING
/// LINK: the one thing a directory lists that no path call finds.
fn stat_operand(mut e: Entry, o: Opts) {
    stat_entry(mut e, o, true)
    if e.kind != a_dangling_link() {
        return
    }
    if !listed_in_parent(e.name) {
        e.kind = -1
    }
}

/// Fill in `e` from the path calls, asking only what `o` needs: every
/// call is a stat, and there is no call that answers more than one
/// thing about a path (wolf-lang#625), so `-t` is one stat per name,
/// `-F` one, `-F -t` two, and a plain listing none at all. `exists`
/// asks whether a file is there at all, which only an operand, `-F`'s
/// `@` and `-L`'s complaint need. A name whose stat finds nothing is
/// taken for a dangling link; `stat_operand` checks that for operands,
/// and inside a listing the directory has just named it.
fn stat_entry(mut e: Entry, o: Opts, exists: bool) {
    e.known = true
    let path = path_of(e)
    e.kind = a_file()
    let size = o.sort == by_size()
    let time = o.sort == by_time()
    if exists || needs_kind(o) {
        if fs_is_dir(path) {
            e.kind = a_directory()
        }
    }
    if size {
        e.size = fs_size(path) else |_| {
            e.kind = a_dangling_link()
            return
        }
    }
    if time {
        e.mtime = fs_modified_ms(path) else |_| {
            e.kind = a_dangling_link()
            return
        }
    }
    if exists && e.kind == a_file() && !size && !time && !fs_exists(path) {
        e.kind = a_dangling_link()
    }
}

/// Whether listing `o`'s way must know a directory from anything else:
/// to put directories first, to mark them, or to descend into them.
fn needs_kind(o: Opts) -> bool {
    o.group_dirs || o.indicator != no_indicator() || o.recursive
}

/// Why `path` cannot be reached, in GNU's words: the host's own reason
/// for a stat of it (`os_error_text`, wolf 0.2.26), so a directory that
/// may not be searched is `Permission denied`. When the stat succeeds or
/// never reached the host (a name the directory does not list), the walk
/// below answers as it did before 0.2.26: `Not a directory` when a
/// leading part of it is a file (`a/`, `a/x`), otherwise `No such file
/// or directory`.
fn missing_reason(path: str) -> str {
    // The host's own answer first (wolf 0.2.26, `[os.fs.error]`): a stat
    // through an unreadable directory is EACCES, `Permission denied`,
    // which the walk below cannot tell from a missing name.
    let code = host_stat_code(path)
    if code != 0 {
        return bore.reason_of(code)
    }
    var at = 1
    while at < path.len {
        let rest = path[at..]
        let slash = rest.find("/") else -1
        if slash < 0 {
            break
        }
        let lead = path[0..at + slash]
        if !fs_exists(lead) {
            break
        }
        if !fs_is_dir(lead) {
            return "Not a directory"
        }
        at += slash + 1
    }
    bore.no_such_file()
}

/// The host's error number for a stat of `path`, or 0 when it succeeds
/// or the host was never asked.
fn host_stat_code(path: str) -> int {
    let _ = fs_size(path) else |_| {
        return bore.host_code()
    }
    0
}

/// Whether the last component of `path` is a name its directory lists.
fn listed_in_parent(path: str) -> bool {
    if path.len == 0 || path.ends_with("/") {
        return false
    }
    let cut = path.rfind("/") else -1
    var dir = "."
    var base = path
    if cut >= 0 {
        dir = if cut == 0 then "/" else path[0..cut]
        base = path[cut + 1..]
    }
    if base == "." || base == ".." {
        return false
    }
    let names = fs_read_dir(dir) else |_| {
        return false
    }
    for n in names {
        if n == base {
            return true
        }
    }
    false
}

/// Whether listing `o`'s way needs every entry stat'ed: to sort by size
/// or time, or to know its directories.
fn needs_stat(o: Opts) -> bool {
    o.sort == by_size() || o.sort == by_time() || needs_kind(o)
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------- sorting

/// The order to print `es` in, stat'ing every entry first when the
/// order or the output needs it.
fn sorted(mut es: List[Entry], o: Opts) -> List[int] {
    if needs_stat(o) {
        var k = 0
        while k < es.len {
            if !es[k].known {
                stat_entry(mut es[k], o, o.indicator == classify() || o.deref == follow_always())
            }
            k += 1
        }
    }
    // A merge sort between two index lists made once, so that sorting
    // n names allocates 2n words however many passes it takes.
    let n = es.len
    var one = List[int]()
    var two = List[int]()
    var i = 0
    while i < n {
        (mut one).push(i)
        (mut two).push(i)
        i += 1
    }
    var run = 1
    var in_one = true
    while run < n {
        if in_one {
            merge_pass(es, o, one, mut two, run)
        } else {
            merge_pass(es, o, two, mut one, run)
        }
        in_one = !in_one
        run *= 2
    }
    if in_one {
        return one
    }
    two
}

/// One pass of the merge: every pair of runs of `run` in `src`, merged
/// into `dst`. Stable, so equal entries keep their order.
fn merge_pass(es: List[Entry], o: Opts, src: List[int], mut dst: List[int], run: int) {
    let n = src.len
    var at = 0
    var lo = 0
    while lo < n {
        let mid = if lo + run < n then lo + run else n
        let hi = if lo + 2 * run < n then lo + 2 * run else n
        var a = lo
        var b = mid
        while a < mid && b < hi {
            if compare(es, o, src[b], src[a]) < 0 {
                dst[at] = src[b]
                b += 1
            } else {
                dst[at] = src[a]
                a += 1
            }
            at += 1
        }
        while a < mid {
            dst[at] = src[a]
            a += 1
            at += 1
        }
        while b < hi {
            dst[at] = src[b]
            b += 1
            at += 1
        }
        lo = hi
    }
}

/// Negative when entry `i` lists before entry `j`.
fn compare(es: List[Entry], o: Opts, i: int, j: int) -> int {
    if o.group_dirs {
        let di = es[i].kind == a_directory()
        let dj = es[j].kind == a_directory()
        if di && !dj {
            return -1
        }
        if dj && !di {
            return 1
        }
    }
    var c = 0
    if o.sort == by_size() {
        c = against(es[j].size, es[i].size)
    } else if o.sort == by_time() {
        c = against(es[j].mtime, es[i].mtime)
    } else if o.sort == by_extension() {
        c = text_order(extension(es[i].name), extension(es[j].name))
    }
    if c == 0 {
        c = text_order(es[i].name, es[j].name)
    }
    if o.reverse {
        return -c
    }
    c
}

fn against(a: int, b: int) -> int {
    if a < b {
        return -1
    }
    if a > b {
        return 1
    }
    0
}

/// Byte order, which is the C locale's (GNU's collation in any other
/// locale is not written: the oracle runs under `LC_ALL=C`).
fn text_order(a: str, b: str) -> int {
    if a < b {
        return -1
    }
    if a > b {
        return 1
    }
    0
}

/// What `-X` sorts by: the text after the last `.`, or nothing.
fn extension(name: str) -> str {
    let dot = name.rfind(".") else -1
    if dot < 0 {
        return ""
    }
    name[dot + 1..]
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- printing

/// One listing (the files among the operands, or one directory) in the
/// chosen format, onto `bytes`. Answers 1 when `-L` met a dangling link
/// it had to follow, else 0.
fn print_entries(mut bytes: List[byte], es: List[Entry], order: List[int], o: Opts) -> int {
    var status = 0
    var items = List[str]()
    var widths = List[int]()
    for at in order {
        let e = copy es[at]
        if o.deref == follow_always() && e.known && e.kind == a_dangling_link() {
            bore.warn("ls", "cannot access {quote_always(path_of(e))}: {bore.no_such_file()}")
            status = 1
        }
        var text = shown(e.name, o.hide)
        var w = width(e.name, o.hide)
        let mark = indicator(e, o)
        if mark.len > 0 {
            text += mark
            w += 1
        }
        (mut items).push(text)
        (mut widths).push(w)
    }
    if o.format == vertical() || o.format == across() {
        for b in bore.grid(items, widths, o.width, o.format == across(), o.tab) {
            (mut bytes).push(b)
        }
    } else if o.format == with_commas() {
        for b in bore.commas(items, widths, o.width) {
            (mut bytes).push(b)
        }
    } else {
        for t in items {
            push_text(mut bytes, t)
            (mut bytes).push(10 as byte)
        }
    }
    status
}

/// `/` after a directory (`-p`, `-F`), `@` after a link (`-F`; only a
/// dangling one can be seen, see the header).
fn indicator(e: Entry, o: Opts) -> str {
    if o.indicator == no_indicator() || !e.known {
        return ""
    }
    if e.kind == a_directory() {
        return "/"
    }
    if e.kind == a_dangling_link() && o.indicator == classify() {
        return "@"
    }
    ""
}

/// A name as it is printed: as it is, or with `-q` every byte outside
/// printable ASCII as `?`.
fn shown(name: str, hide: bool) -> str {
    if !hide {
        return name
    }
    var plain = true
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if !printable(b as int) {
            plain = false
        }
    }
    if plain {
        return name
    }
    var out = List[byte]()
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if printable(b as int) {
            (mut out).push(b)
        } else {
            (mut out).push(63 as byte)
        }
    }
    str_from_utf8(out) else name
}

/// The columns a name takes, as GNU counts them in the C locale: one
/// per printable byte, and an unprintable one counts nothing printed
/// as it is, one shown as `?`.
fn width(name: str, hide: bool) -> int {
    var w = 0
    for b in name.bytes() {
        if hide || printable(b as int) {
            w += 1
        }
    }
    w
}

fn printable(n: int) -> bool {
    n >= 32 && n < 127
}

/// A name quoted as GNU quotes one it could not reach: always quoted,
/// in the style `bore.quote_name` writes.
fn quote_always(name: str) -> str {
    let q = bore.quote_name(name)
    if q == name {
        return "'{name}'"
    }
    q
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- the vocabulary

/// Every option GNU's `ls` 9.11 has, served or refused. The LONG names
/// are in the order GNU lists them when an abbreviation is ambiguous
/// (`--d` names five, measured), because `bore.parse` lists them in
/// this table's order.
fn options() -> List[bore.Opt] {
    var s = List[bore.Opt]()
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("a", "all"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("A", "almost-all"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "author"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("b", "escape"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "block-size"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "classify"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "color"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("Z", "context"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("d", "directory"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("D", "dired"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("H", "dereference-command-line"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "dereference-command-line-symlink-to-dir"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("L", "dereference"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("h", "human-readable"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("q", "hide-control-chars"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "hide"))
    (mut s).push(bore.optional("", "hyperlink"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "help"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("s", "size"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "si"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "show-control-chars"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "sort"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("T", "tabsize"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "time"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "time-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("i", "inode"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("B", "ignore-backups"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("I", "ignore"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "indicator-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "full-time"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "file-type"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "format"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("Q", "quote-name"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("", "quoting-style"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("n", "numeric-uid-gid"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("G", "no-group"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("r", "reverse"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("R", "recursive"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "group-directories-first"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("k", "kibibytes"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("N", "literal"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("w", "width"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "zero"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "version"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("c", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("C", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("f", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("F", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("g", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("l", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("m", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("o", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("p", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("S", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("t", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("u", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("U", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("v", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("x", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("X", ""))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("1", ""))
    s
}

/// `--format`'s words in GNU's order; the meanings are the `format`
/// numbers above.
fn format_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("verbose")
    (mut s).push("long")
    (mut s).push("commas")
    (mut s).push("horizontal")
    (mut s).push("across")
    (mut s).push("vertical")
    (mut s).push("single-column")
    s
}

fn format_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(long_format())
    (mut s).push(long_format())
    (mut s).push(with_commas())
    (mut s).push(across())
    (mut s).push(across())
    (mut s).push(vertical())
    (mut s).push(one_per_line())
    s
}

fn sort_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("size")
    (mut s).push("time")
    (mut s).push("version")
    (mut s).push("extension")
    (mut s).push("name")
    (mut s).push("width")
    s
}

fn sort_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(unsorted())
    (mut s).push(by_size())
    (mut s).push(by_time())
    (mut s).push(by_version())
    (mut s).push(by_extension())
    (mut s).push(by_name())
    (mut s).push(by_width())
    s
}

/// `--time`'s words; only `mtime` (0) is served.
fn time_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("atime")
    (mut s).push("access")
    (mut s).push("use")
    (mut s).push("ctime")
    (mut s).push("status")
    (mut s).push("mtime")
    (mut s).push("modification")
    (mut s).push("birth")
    (mut s).push("creation")
    s
}

fn time_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(3)
    (mut s).push(3)
    s
}

/// The WHEN of `--color` and `--classify`: 0 always, 1 never, 2 when
/// standard output is a terminal, which it is never taken to be here.
fn when_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("always")
    (mut s).push("yes")
    (mut s).push("force")
    (mut s).push("never")
    (mut s).push("no")
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("auto")
    (mut s).push("tty")
    (mut s).push("if-tty")
    s
}

fn when_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(0)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(1)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    (mut s).push(2)
    s
}

fn indicator_words() -> List[str] {
    var s = List[str]()
    (mut s).push("none")
    (mut s).push("slash")
    (mut s).push("file-type")
    (mut s).push("classify")
    s
}

fn indicator_meanings() -> List[int] {
    var s = List[int]()
    (mut s).push(no_indicator())
    (mut s).push(slash())
    (mut s).push(3)
    (mut s).push(classify())
    s
}

fn help() -> str {
    """
    Usage: ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...
    List each FILE, and the contents of each directory FILE (the
    working directory when there is none), sorted by name.

    Which names:
      -a, --all                  every name, the ones starting with . too
      -A, --almost-all           every name but . and ..
      -d, --directory            the operands themselves, not what they hold
      -R, --recursive            every directory under each one as well

    How they are laid out:
      -1                         one name per line (the default here)
      -C                         in columns, filled top to bottom
      -x                         in columns, filled left to right
      -m                         on lines, separated by commas
          --format=WORD          vertical (-C), across or horizontal (-x),
                                 commas (-m), single-column (-1)
      -w, --width=COLS           the line width for -C, -x and -m
                                 (0: no limit; else COLUMNS, else 80)
      -T, --tabsize=COLS         a tab stop every COLS columns (0: no tabs)

    In what order (by name, in byte order, unless):
      -S                         largest first
      -t                         newest modification first
      -X                         by the text after the last .
          --sort=WORD            name, size (-S), time (-t), extension (-X)
          --time=mtime           sort by modification time
      -r, --reverse              the other way round
          --group-directories-first  directories before everything else

    What is shown:
      -F, --classify             / after a directory, @ after a link
          --file-type            the same
      -p                         / after a directory
          --indicator-style=WORD none, slash (-p), file-type, classify (-F)
      -q, --hide-control-chars   ? for every byte that is not printable
          --show-control-chars   names as they are (the default here)
      -h, -k                     accepted; they change only -l and -s

    Links:
      -H, --dereference-command-line  follow a link given as an operand
      -L, --dereference          follow every link

          --help                 print this help and exit
          --version              print the version and exit

    Not here yet, and refused by name: -l and what implies it (-g -n -o
    --full-time), -i, -s, -u, -c, -U, -f, -v, and the quoting, colour
    and filtering options. This ls never asks whether its output is a
    terminal, so it always writes what GNU's writes into a pipe.

    Exit status: 0 when all went well, 1 for a minor problem (a
    subdirectory it could not open), 2 for a serious one (an operand it
    could not reach, a bad option).

    boreutils: https://github.com/wolffe-lang/boreutils
    """
}
